on/math-academy-id "q-101587" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=5{e}^{x}+1$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $1$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(5{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =5\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =5{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=0} & ={(5{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =5{e}^{0} \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237850 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d31247c-4643-5464-a30c-802705aaf50b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236007} {:answer/id #uuid "3b878615-e206-52a0-bf52-3f4f50ce31b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236190} {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} {:answer/id #uuid "9e126482-0792-53fb-9f81-3febb0cec7c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236807} {:answer/id #uuid "a16f91bd-4ea9-5f5b-b438-3d185dbe8f3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54afb277-4dae-5d46-82a6-d2ef3ba15dd9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236358} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ea4542f-4e0e-5b2c-a84d-fa40f33689f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237504}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cf78a5ba-4334-5b57-bc16-3a848e2f0dba" :question/math-academy-id "q-101590" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡11) \\\\  & =2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(2{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =2{e}^{1} \\\\  & =2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314904 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312717} {:answer/id #uuid "6cffecb4-1fcc-411a-8289-8bcd57cb4ab4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186409337}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cdb78ad-20bf-5af0-a094-1b3f6357e552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312717} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a411b7f-e09b-54e6-9330-1c2c71a4efc6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314334}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6869b95a-63a5-54db-b062-b375ad3ac68d" :question/math-academy-id "q-340056" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8,$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x}+ln⁡8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(6{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡8) \\\\  & =6\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =6{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186314906 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6db64c05-bd48-51ef-9218-df180caf5534" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186312840} :answer-field/id #uuid "413c0c5a-e837-526a-8b03-fa256fc8b859" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186314186}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "19b7181e-4102-59d9-864a-c870a873bbda" :question/math-academy-id "q-340067" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡4$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x}+ln⁡4) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡4) \\\\  & =9\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =9{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡2} & ={(9{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9{e}^{ln⁡2} \\\\  & =9\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338886 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2615d5d0-577d-51e9-a7eb-7299c88c1240" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336388} {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} {:answer/id #uuid "935cffee-f030-54ce-8980-0ae960bd62a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337376} {:answer/id #uuid "b4aa127c-c35b-5236-91c3-effa79e34c62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=10-{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337635} {:answer/id #uuid "d6f526af-9e12-5d00-a48a-09643a747fff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337899}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d4ee843-f5a5-5e6f-a7b4-d39ee29e9a08" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{f}^{′}(x)=-2{e}^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336465} :answer-field/id #uuid "c72ca8b2-1291-5b13-840e-71b284db3d8b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338635}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bd4ec376-93b4-54c8-9a98-7640cf3d729e" :question/math-academy-id "q-71053" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=10-2{e}^{x},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $10$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(10)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0-2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c81a280-e468-5bb1-ae24-06ecd33d692c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0c81a280-e468-5bb1-ae24-06ecd33d692c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480007} :answer-field/id #uuid "369e2fe5-a4b2-5cf1-a353-9ab634eb9000" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480012}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24285fdc-6262-5d37-b204-181d4efdc6b1" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡3$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡3$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡3} & ={(- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480009 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "548f8af3-6988-5dcf-b7db-7042ec33f9ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2e^{x} + \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480018} {:answer/id #uuid "8d5c6f98-32c7-5e01-876f-dd32bf0f5f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480029} {:answer/id #uuid "9cfe2a01-f83d-5791-a0bb-9056c4f88b26" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480031} {:answer/id #uuid "b992cfc5-1e95-5320-aa79-472613633d31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = \\ln 2 \\cdot e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480036} {:answer/id #uuid "dff80c7e-a9c2-5066-8ca4-139ee627d565" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d5c6f98-32c7-5e01-876f-dd32bf0f5f97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "f^{′}(x) = 2e^{x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480029} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1376578-3b74-543a-904f-628166133f63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480033}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "27963039-7ab9-50a1-a524-335dda2120d3" :question/problem "Given that $f(x) = \\ln 2 + 2e^{x}$, find $f^{′}(x)$." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nUsing the addition rule, then, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2+2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡2)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5cf23329-5460-51e6-917d-f464bb0d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5cf23329-5460-51e6-917d-f464bb0d4626" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480022} :answer-field/id #uuid "d50e007d-5e38-5d66-ac2c-89d4723aae0b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480042}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3368f5c9-3775-5d06-9195-e0e05c5824a2" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480014 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31e88a41-78ca-580b-a312-79116f88663a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480010} {:answer/id #uuid "3bcde689-7645-5ddd-989e-d3c8f722028e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480013} {:answer/id #uuid "50eade78-4e28-509e-9c5d-091c94140008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480015} {:answer/id #uuid "84a258fd-c8f6-529f-a8a2-a5a143aa098e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\ln{\\left(9 \\right)} + 7 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480028} {:answer/id #uuid "bc11084f-7105-5e40-8cf2-b58e3962a347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480038}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc11084f-7105-5e40-8cf2-b58e3962a347" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480038} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0428780-07b4-5eb1-a923-27ac73163009" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480046}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "40eee14f-7f06-5ca0-8856-ee0dc15c1493" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}$, find the slope of the tangent at $x=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-ln⁡9$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9+7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-ln⁡9)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(7{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+7\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =7{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=1} & ={(7{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=1} \\\\  & =7{e}^{1} \\\\  & =7 e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78806f5e-a59a-5036-8a8a-e637dbb300bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480026}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "78806f5e-a59a-5036-8a8a-e637dbb300bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{18}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480026} :answer-field/id #uuid "56a8a426-86a3-500f-b11d-f1d5bdfd0d6b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480019}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5104b80b-063d-53dc-91b3-3f3e99aa0687" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡9$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡9} & ={(- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}{e}^{-ln⁡9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9} \\\\  & =- \\frac{5}{18}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480017 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69473c5c-ae42-5677-bf46-38ec4c571473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480024}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69473c5c-ae42-5677-bf46-38ec4c571473" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480024} :answer-field/id #uuid "59172bf7-c76f-53d2-a87d-8c7ee520c969" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480021}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5184f338-d6d0-533d-88bb-8b37da109e6c" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡2} & ={(- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{4}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =- \\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480023 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5c9cce5-6b1c-514b-83d7-a145c5acd6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480045}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e5c9cce5-6b1c-514b-83d7-a145c5acd6a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{36}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480045} :answer-field/id #uuid "ba6843cd-87ec-513b-bc48-aa3b5a960609" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480037}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5f698a42-ae62-5802-8f77-88360f877560" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-8$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-8) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} & ={(\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{12}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{3}\\cdot \\frac{5}{12} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480027 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf34f77f-125e-5a29-9908-3c47b6785d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480040}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf34f77f-125e-5a29-9908-3c47b6785d3d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480040} :answer-field/id #uuid "f05ed301-7707-5213-90a6-1584eb6e88ca" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480047}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "78a2b5f7-5cef-5012-a53c-264a4e19606b" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5+\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} & ={(\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{ln⁡\\frac{4}{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5726971d-109b-59ca-a9ae-86038320a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480020}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5726971d-109b-59ca-a9ae-86038320a437" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{21}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480020} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdae0fc4-9547-5bff-bd8c-baecad3058a1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b423e41e-b9ca-54b7-896b-f125c7eb77b4" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $2$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} & ={(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{2}{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{21}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480041 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6dc1213d-28ba-50b4-a7d5-d912d5920958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480025}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6dc1213d-28ba-50b4-a7d5-d912d5920958" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{16}{55}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480025} :answer-field/id #uuid "9cd6ad36-c3a9-5988-b038-55ae25095101" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cf08154e-5ad6-5738-b7be-7d9a74b0856c" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{11}{2}$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡7$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}+ln⁡7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡7) \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡\\frac{11}{2}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{11}{2}} & ={(- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}{e}^{-ln⁡\\frac{11}{2}} \\\\  & =- \\frac{8}{5}\\cdot \\frac{2}{11} \\\\  & =- \\frac{16}{55}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480044 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "033b9386-0f9d-5940-814c-e123532dbf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "033b9386-0f9d-5940-814c-e123532dbf04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480005} :answer-field/id #uuid "0618abee-de32-5956-948c-7d1ee4894cd0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e471cfb8-b421-5129-be11-398f77d53a45" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=ln⁡5+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡2$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $ln⁡5$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5+\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(ln⁡5)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{7}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡2} & ={(\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}{e}^{-ln⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186480048 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bb40a0ff-c3b5-512d-a436-cc2fce456008" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d8c6b79-f2e6-53a0-a862-d058db79b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480032}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d8c6b79-f2e6-53a0-a862-d058db79b1df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480032} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8bb0ea8-e867-5b13-8fba-c0a5615adfbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186480035}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f32ccf8c-9eba-53b7-a331-a184e3771a1b" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)=\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}-11$, the slope of the tangent line at $x=-ln⁡5$ is{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "The derivative of ${e}^{x}$ is just ${e}^{x}$ itself. On the other hand, $-11$ is a constant, so its derivative is $0.$\n\nFirst, using the addition rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-11) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent at $x=-ln⁡5$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=-ln⁡5} & ={(\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{x})\\,|}_{x=-ln⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}{e}^{-ln⁡5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating the Product of a Constant and the Exponential Function, Plus a Constant"}]}
